The President can correct a principal's stage and delete either record

Asked for after the first pass over the screens.

§5c's stage machine is forward-only, which is right for the ordinary path and means a stage
entered by mistake was permanent — and a record that cannot be corrected is one where people
start keeping the real answer somewhere else. So the President can now set any stage, backwards
or skipping, with a mandatory reason. Deliberately not granted to `principal_prospect.manage`:
the forward-only order is what makes the pipeline mean anything, and a rule everybody can step
around is not a rule.

The override does not emit principal.stage_changed. Subscribers treat that as the pipeline
moving; a correction is somebody fixing the record, and an override into `appointed` firing it
would create a supplier behind the officers' backs — the one decision §5c reserves to them.

Deletes for both, soft, with a reason. §2 makes the supplier directory deliberately easy to add
to — "fast and forgiving, it is the only way suppliers get in" — which means duplicates and typos
get in too, and until now nothing could take one out. Both refuse when something downstream would
be left pointing at nothing: a prospect already converted into a supplier, a supplier with a
purchase order or price request against it. The supplier delete unlinks its prospect, because
supplierId is the guard that makes §5c's conversion idempotent and leaving it set would make that
prospect permanently unconvertible.

Both under new permissions granted to the President alone — narrower than appointing or
approving, which the Vice President shares.

Also fixes a test that had been leaking into the company's database: customer-po.test.ts walks an
inquiry to `quoting`, which makes module 02 raise a real draft quotation from the live counter,
and nothing was tracking it. The account leak in the same file is not yet traced and is noted.

794 tests across 84 files pass with the dev server stopped — which also confirms the queue-test
failure in the previous run was the dev drainer claiming a job mid-test, not a regression.

